-- title:
--   Level-one node ring has fraction field fieldOver(q,K)
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, a field $k$ of characteristic $q$ and a ring homomorphism $\mathrm{red} : A \to k$; fix modular polynomial data `data` for $q$ (a monic bivariate integral polynomial of degree $\psi(q)$ killing the pair $(j,j_q)$) together with a proof `hKr` that its reduction modulo $q$ equals $(X'^q - X)(X' - X^q)$, and proofs $h\alpha$, $h\beta$ that the Hecke maps $\alpha$, $\beta$ at level $1$ and prime $q$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ are integral ring homomorphisms. Let $P$ be a place specialisation for these data at level $N = 1$ and $R$ a prolongation tuple over $P$, with $k$ algebraically closed. Let $K$ be an intermediate field of $\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q$ with $[K:\mathbb Q] < \infty$, and let $w$ be a place of the level-one function field $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\,k\,1$ satisfying the predicate `IsSupersingularPlace` for $q$ at level $1$. Finally let $z$ be a Laurent series over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ lying in the subfield $\mathrm{NodeLocalized.fieldOver}\,(1\cdot q)\,K$, that is, the subfield generated by the constant series attached to elements of $K$ together with $j(\mathfrak q)$ and $j(\mathfrak q^{1\cdot q})$. Then there exist $x,y$ in the base-changed full modular function field $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,(1\cdot q)$, both lying in $R.\mathrm{nodeIntegersOver}\,K\,w$ — i.e. both in $R.\mathrm{nodeIntegers}\,w$ and with Laurent expansions in $\mathrm{fieldOver}\,(1\cdot q)\,K$ — with $y \neq 0$ and $z \cdot y = x$ as Laurent series.
--
--   This is the level-one case of the statement that $K(j(\mathfrak q), j(\mathfrak q^{q}))$ is the fraction field of the $K$-rational node ring of $X_0(q)$ at a supersingular node, in the form of a divisibility statement with explicit numerator and denominator. It feeds the component-group computation for principal divisors at level one, being cited in the proof that the projection of a depth-dual plus degree combination vanishes for $q \ge 5$ dividing the level-one data.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_PlaceSpecialization_ProlongationTuple_exists_mul_eq_of_mem_fieldOver_nodeIntegersOver_levelOne.lean

